## ABSTRACT

This paper presents an anthropological genealogy of formalization, tracing the descent of binary systems from their origins in moral evaluation through their evolution into ethics, spirituality, religion, and logic. We demonstrate that the binary structure of logic (true/false) originates in the fundamental moral binary (good/evil), which itself emerges from the survival imperative (helpful/destructive). The evidence is anthropological: every human culture exhibits this sequence of formalization. We prove that morals are the source of the original binary, ethics is the formalization of shared values, spirituality derives from the need for hope, religion is the formalization of spirituality, and logic is derived from ethics. This genealogy reveals that logic's binary nature is not a discovery about reality but an inheritance from moral evaluation, formalized through successive layers of abstraction. The framework provides a complete account of the origin, development, and proper role of formal systems in human reasoning.

