## 1. INTRODUCTION

### 1.1 The Anthropological Observation

The observation is empirical and anthropological:

> **All human cultures exhibit the same sequence of formalization.**
> **The sequence is observable in the archaeological record.**
> **The sequence is observable in ethnographic data.**
> **The sequence is observable in cognitive development.**

The sequence is:

```
MORAL EVALUATION (Binary: Helpful/Destructive)
    ↓
ETHICS (Formalization of shared moral values)
    ↓
SPIRITUALITY (Hope in the face of uncertainty)
    ↓
RELIGION (Formalization of spirituality)
    ↓
LOGIC (Formalization of reasoning)
```

### 1.2 The Central Claim

The central claim is:

> **The binary is not discovered in nature.**
> **The binary is inherited from morals.**
> **Morals are the source of the original binary.**
> **All subsequent binaries are formalizations of this original binary.**

This is not a philosophical claim. This is an **anthropological observation**. The evidence is:

1. **Prehistoric burials** show moral evaluation (care for the dead)
2. **Paleolithic art** shows shared values (aesthetics, symbolism)
3. **Shamanic practices** show spirituality (hope, healing, meaning)
4. **Early temples** show religion (formalized spirituality)
5. **Written law codes** show formalized ethics
6. **Philosophical treatises** show formalized logic

